1
00:00:03,628 --> 00:00:06,008
Yeah, I miss you too, sweetie. Ha, ha.

2
00:00:06,172 --> 00:00:08,842
Okay, listen, I gotta go,
but I'll see you tonight.

3
00:00:09,009 --> 00:00:10,969
(Howard(on the phone):)Bye-bye. Yeah, bye-bye.

4
00:00:11,136 --> 00:00:13,926
(Howard(on the phone):)No, you hang up first.

5
00:00:14,681 --> 00:00:16,181
(Howard(on the phone):)Hello?

6
00:00:17,809 --> 00:00:19,979
(Raj:)Dude, I'm glad
you finally got a girlfriend...

7
00:00:20,145 --> 00:00:23,435
...but do you have to do lovey-dovey
in front of us who don't?

8
00:00:23,606 --> 00:00:25,186
(Sheldon:)Actually, he might have to.

9
00:00:25,358 --> 00:00:28,488
(Sheldon:)There's an economic concept
known as a "positional good"...

10
00:00:28,653 --> 00:00:32,863
(Sheldon:)...in which an object is only valued
because it's not possessed by others.

11
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:36,493
(Sheldon:)The term was coined in 1976
by economist Fred Hirsch...

12
00:00:36,661 --> 00:00:40,711
(Sheldon:)...to replace the more colloquial
but less precise "neener-neener."

13
00:00:40,874 --> 00:00:44,164
(Howard:)That's not true. My happiness
is not dependent on my best friend...

14
00:00:44,335 --> 00:00:46,545
(Howard:)-...being miserable and alone.
-Thank you.

15
00:00:46,713 --> 00:00:48,713
I'd be lying if I said it wasn't a perk.

16
00:00:50,008 --> 00:00:51,838
(Leonard:)-Who's miserable and alone?
-Me.

17
00:00:52,010 --> 00:00:55,390
(Leonard:)Oh. I used to be like that.
Then I got a girlfriend.

18
00:00:56,598 --> 00:01:00,268
In pre-1976 terms, "neener-neener."

19
00:01:00,769 --> 00:01:03,349
(Leonard:)What are you and Bernadette doing
for Valentine's Day?

